WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.480
Hey folks, I’ve got a bunch of projects in the&nbsp;
works cooking up for later, and I’ve been loving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:10.240
Edge of Eternities so far, so I figured this was&nbsp;
a lovely time for a precon review. I put it up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.880
a vote, and my Patrons narrowly chose the World&nbsp;
Shaper precon, so that’s what we’re going to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.920
at in this video. Here’s the format—I’m going to&nbsp;
tell you what I think this deck is trying to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.160
I’m going to talk about the areas it succeeds&nbsp;
in, and then we can talk about its weaknesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.680
and a set of card swaps that could&nbsp;
help out with those. To start with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:34.240
let’s talk about the commander. Hearthhull, the&nbsp;
Worldseed is a 4 mana Spacecraft in Jund colors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.200
with Spacecraft being a new artifact subtype&nbsp;
that’s being introduced in Edge of Eternity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.840
They’re artifacts that can basically be leveled&nbsp;
up by tapping creatures to gain more abilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:49.600
similar to crewing a vehicle, and a lot of them&nbsp;
eventually become creatures as well. This set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.320
also brings a rules change to the commander format&nbsp;
where any legendary vehicle or spacecraft with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.960
power/toughness box can be in the command zone,&nbsp;
and over on the podcast I cohost we just did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.040
a tier list of all vehicle commanders, which&nbsp;
will be linked down in the description. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:08.480
so that’s spacecraft, but what does Hearthhull in&nbsp;
particular do? Nothing at first, but once it’s had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:14.080
two power put into it it gains the ability to tap,&nbsp;
pay 1, and sacrifice a land to draw two cards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:19.280
gain an additional land play this turn. At eight&nbsp;
charge counters, it becomes a 6/7 with flying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.760
vigilance, haste, and “Whenever you sacrifice&nbsp;
a land, each opponent loses two life.”

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.200
Initial impressions—this seems like a solid value&nbsp;
engine with some explosive endgame utility. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.520
we squint a bit, we can kind of think of its&nbsp;
first ability as having a floor of tapping to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.920
draw a card, since we’ll probably draw and play&nbsp;
another land from those two cards pretty often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.800
repaying the 1 mana if it entered untapped.&nbsp;
This is a fine starting point, but obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.520
there are a whole lot of things that amplify&nbsp;
the power of this. The biggest one is things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.400
that can utilize lands in our graveyard—if we’re&nbsp;
able to get them back out into play in some form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.880
this card represents a fair amount of ramp&nbsp;
potential in addition to the value it provides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.960
Another amplifier would be landfall effects&nbsp;
that benefit from additional lands entering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.520
and third would be payoffs for permanents being&nbsp;
sacrificed, and if we can get some number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.440
these online the 2+ ability on Hearthhull will&nbsp;
pack a ton of utility. The 8+ modality is a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.520
less appealing to me to get to early on, since&nbsp;
it makes our commander more removable without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.840
putting on much pressure in most cases, but&nbsp;
it’s a nice tool to have in our toolbox as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:25.520
something that can be turned on right before a&nbsp;
big land-sacrifice play. Overall, a nice package.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.920
So, how does this deck work with this&nbsp;
commander? Well there are six cards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.560
can recur multiple lands from the graveyard,&nbsp;
three instantaneous and three more gradual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.800
along with a number of other cards that&nbsp;
can sacrifice lands to perform effects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.840
This, to me, represents a core ramp engine of the&nbsp;
deck—sacrificing lands to perform various effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.560
before spitting those sacrificed lands back out&nbsp;
into play, building a lot of mana and value in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.760
the process. Mana and value don’t win games,&nbsp;
though, so what are our payoffs for futzing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:59.840
with lands? Three main things—four landfall&nbsp;
cards, nine payoffs for sacrificing permanents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.320
and four cards that check the number of lands&nbsp;
in graveyards, with most of these payoff cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:09.040
amplifying a player’s board or pushing damage&nbsp;
in some way. To support this gameplan, there’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.400
pretty wide and diverse removal suite, as well as&nbsp;
a deliciously high land count of 42. So to recap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.920
this deck is trying to build mana and value&nbsp;
through the process of sacrificing and replaying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:23.040
lands before turning things up to 11 with a suite&nbsp;
of permanents that perform powerful effects when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.480
parts of this cycle happen. The endgame plan is&nbsp;
likely going to be a mixture of large creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.000
moderate-sized creature tokens, and&nbsp;
potentially an explosive burst of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.800
life loss from the commander if things line just&nbsp;
right. So, what do I think about this gameplan?

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.280
Let’s talk about what I like about this deck, and&nbsp;
since this is a lands deck, I’m gonna mix things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.080
up and talk about the deck’s manabase first.&nbsp;
Overall, there’s quite a lot to be excited about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.280
here. First of all, 42 lands is a fair amount, but&nbsp;
with a commander that both sacrifices lands and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.480
gives extra land plays, this number is perfectly&nbsp;
reasonable. In terms of raw manabase quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:00.880
things are pretty great here—great symbol counts&nbsp;
in black and green and a fine enough symbol count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:05.520
in red given that it’s a secondary color. Like&nbsp;
the last precon I reviewed, we have a moderately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.080
large count of taplands here, but given the high&nbsp;
total land count, I don’t see this really being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:15.520
an issue. More to the point of what’s cool about&nbsp;
this manabase, there’s an impressive density of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:20.800
budget fetches here—a whole 10! I’ll admit that&nbsp;
I was at first a little skeptical of the the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.440
tapped fetches and especially the three Capenna&nbsp;
fetches here, but these start to make a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.880
sense when you remember that the four most&nbsp;
powerful land recursion spells in the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.720
return the lands to play tapped, which isn’t a&nbsp;
downside for any of these five. And the upside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:40.480
of these is immense—these tie into all three&nbsp;
of our main payoff types, those being lands in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.280
graveyard payoffs, permanent sacrificing payoffs,&nbsp;
and landfall effects. The amount of utility these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.880
subtly add to the deck is really cool. Finally&nbsp;
let’s talk about the typed duals—we have all three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.760
of both tango lands and bicycle lands, which can&nbsp;
be fetched with any card grabbing a basic-typed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:01.360
land. And yes, that includes the Golgari ones as&nbsp;
well—the commander team at Wizards is continuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:07.120
in their quest to flesh out dual land cycles in&nbsp;
precons, and I’m super here for it. These are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.320
lovely for any players who want dual types&nbsp;
and don’t want to shell out for one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.200
more expensive options—even just having a little&nbsp;
extra upside over a basic tapland is really nice.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.760
Okay, so how does this deck build off of this&nbsp;
strong foundation of lands? Let’s talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.640
the core engine here—this deck is looking to&nbsp;
sacrifice lands, oftentimes recur those lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.640
and generally get payoffs from the act of playing&nbsp;
and sacrificing lands. In practice, this mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.040
works out. If there’s one thing this deck can&nbsp;
do, it’s get lands into the graveyard—between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:40.480
the commander, 10 fetches, and 13 cards in the 99&nbsp;
that sacrifice lands, this deck is very consistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:45.040
at finding ways to fill its graveyard with lands,&nbsp;
should it want to do such a thing. For recurring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.840
lands, there’s a somewhat lower quantity,&nbsp;
though this is to be expected for a payoff card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:53.760
and the deck for the most part seems high-velocity&nbsp;
enough to find these—it tends to churn through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:59.520
lot of cards. When they are found, the impact&nbsp;
can be big—even relatively early in the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.920
finding one of the deck’s three mass land&nbsp;
recursion cards can routinely grab 4 or more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:08.160
lands. I ran a number of playtests with this&nbsp;
deck out to the end of turn 5, and there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:13.360
a surprising number of them where I finished with&nbsp;
10 or even 12 lands in play, which can set up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.720
deck for some serious midgame shenanigans if&nbsp;
it can keep finding its way into nonland plays.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:23.760
How about payoffs outside of land recursion?&nbsp;
As I mentioned earlier, two major payoff types&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.560
are landfall and permanent sacrifice, and&nbsp;
these both play pretty cool roles here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:32.640
particularly the former. Landfall is a&nbsp;
great tool here because it doesn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:37.040
require much extra setup—with the deck’s 10&nbsp;
fetchlands and extra land play commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:42.240
you’re already looking at anywhere from 2-4&nbsp;
lands entering per turn, and that’s before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.520
taking into account ramp or land recursion. There&nbsp;
are three powerful finishers utilizing this fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:52.160
as well as Tireless Tracker as a lovely value&nbsp;
engine. Permanent-sacrifice payoffs exist in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:57.280
somewhat similar realm—the commander sacrifices a&nbsp;
land every turn, and tack a single fetchland per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:01.920
turn on top of that and you’re looking at two&nbsp;
easy triggers per turn, before considering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:06.720
13 nonland cards that can help with this task.&nbsp;
A lot of these payoffs are pretty sweet—you’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.520
got classic Jund powerhouses like Korvold and&nbsp;
Mayhem Devil, as well as some neat new ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.000
printed for this precon. I have opinions about&nbsp;
the exact quantity of these here that I’ll get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.920
to a little later, but the bottom line here&nbsp;
is that I really like this structuring for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:24.720
the deck. This deck will be at its best when&nbsp;
it’s able to perform land recursion, but most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:28.880
of these landfall and sacrifice payoff cards have&nbsp;
a high enough floor that they’ll feel perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:34.960
fine even if the deck is currently operating at a&nbsp;
somewhat lower caliber. This, to me, is ideal for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:40.400
a precon—you want to give newer players a chance&nbsp;
to pop off and be the big bad sometimes, but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:44.560
also want their deck to chug along and make them&nbsp;
feel like they’re getting to play even if they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:49.520
not having some sort of big gamer turn, and most&nbsp;
of this deck’s payoffs accomplish that goal.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.760
Moving on to this deck’s removal suite, things&nbsp;
continue to look pretty solid—five pieces of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.560
instant speed, single-target removal, five pieces&nbsp;
of multi-removal, and then another seven cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.200
that perform some sort of hybrid role, being a&nbsp;
removal engine or performing another task as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.280
I’ll highlight some favorites here, but the key&nbsp;
takeaway here is that this removal suite is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.520
pretty great, and there’s only one card here&nbsp;
I find even remotely worth cutting. This deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:16.160
is obviously a big Mayhem Devil W, and I also&nbsp;
love Windgrace’s Judgement and Pest Infestation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:20.720
as powerful multi-removal options in decks that&nbsp;
can afford to pay up a bit of mana. There’s two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.160
pieces of graveyard exile here, but it might&nbsp;
feel like a lot more in practice since this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:29.680
deck is high-velocity and has the capability to&nbsp;
loop Bojuka Bog to its heart’s content. We also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:35.520
have to talk about Planetary Annihilation—this&nbsp;
is a wild new design, dealing 6 damage to each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.640
creature and forcing each player to sacrifice down&nbsp;
to 6 lands. To discuss the card’s merits here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.000
it works really satisfyingly with both the&nbsp;
commander and this deck’s sources of mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.680
land recursion, and outside of that I was&nbsp;
nothing short of electrified to see this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.720
sort of effect printed in a precon in the first&nbsp;
place. I wear the conflicting hats of big mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:56.960
player and advocate for incomplete mana wipes&nbsp;
such as this, and while I don’t want to derail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:00.400
this video with a whole by jumping away to talk&nbsp;
about that for five minutes, I did discuss my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:04.000
thoughts on this card and some others from&nbsp;
Edge of Eternity in a segment available to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:07.840
Patrons and YouTube members, and there will&nbsp;
be a preview of that at the end of the video.

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:12.400
Okay, so that’s what I like about this deck—a&nbsp;
fair amount. But what do I dislike about this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:19.120
precon? Only really two things—it lacks early game&nbsp;
utility plays, and it has too much land sacrifice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.920
and sacrifice payoff, especially considering that&nbsp;
the commander is both of these things already.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:27.680
So, the way we’re gonna go about improving this&nbsp;
is that we’re going to cut some of the trickier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:32.240
payoffs and replace them with other payoff&nbsp;
cards that are more flexible, and we’re also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:36.000
going to add a handful of cards for purposes of&nbsp;
smoothing the deck’s early game and helping with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.360
setup. Unlike basically all of my previous precon&nbsp;
reviews, I’m not really going to be introducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.800
any new elements to the deck—the ideas are already&nbsp;
here, we just need to commit a bit harder to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:52.400
better choices in the deck. Let’s start with&nbsp;
cuts. First, Loamcrafter Faun—unless you just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.960
want an outlet to discard lands, which I don’t&nbsp;
think this deck wants, the two requirements to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:03.040
make the ETB good won’t be lining up often enough.&nbsp;
Scouring Swarm is honestly just a pretty mediocre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:07.600
card—obviously flying is nice and there’s a&nbsp;
bit of explosive potential, but each of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:12.160
conditions are too difficult to meet to justify&nbsp;
their respective payoffs, and it doesn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:17.520
have 2 power to get Hearthhull juiced up in one&nbsp;
go. World Breaker costs way too much mana for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:22.320
the utility it provides this deck, so it’s also&nbsp;
gotta go. Sprouting Goblin at least accomplishes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:27.280
a couple tasks the deck vaguely wants, but the&nbsp;
overall package here is still a bit meh. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:31.440
I sort of see the vision with Groundskeeper&nbsp;
and I’d be okay with it if it was a 2/1, but as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:37.040
things stand the use cases for it just aren’t wide&nbsp;
enough. Uurg, Spawn of Turg sadly just doesn’t do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:42.960
enough for my liking, being neither a great early&nbsp;
game play nor a particularly ideal later play.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:47.600
Finally, this deck is just a bit overloaded on&nbsp;
higher cost and trickier to use payoffs, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:54.240
two I’m choosing to cut here are Mazirek, Kraul&nbsp;
Death Priest and Centaur Vinecrasher. If you love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:58.720
those cards, I dunno, pretend I named two other&nbsp;
payoff cards you like a bit less. The specifics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:03.760
aren’t that important for these last two cuts,&nbsp;
we’re just trying to rebalance the ratios a bit.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:09.920
Now onto the additions—first of all and by orders&nbsp;
of magnitude most importantly, Spelunking. Land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:14.240
untapping effects are disgustingly powerful in&nbsp;
this deck, and the options out there other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.520
Spelunking are quite pricey. Next up, I did cut&nbsp;
some land-sacrificing payoffs, and the logical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:25.440
replacement to me is landfall. Lotus Cobra and&nbsp;
Tireless Provisioner are both great mana engines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:29.680
and Valakut Exploration is a great value&nbsp;
engine that doubles as a damage wincon in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.160
a big land-fiddling turn, potentially working in&nbsp;
tandem with the commander to one-shot opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:39.520
from shockingly high life totals. Next up, I wanna&nbsp;
get some higher-utility two drops in here. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:44.080
deck has a lot of late game potential, but is&nbsp;
a little clunky right now and hungry for early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:48.560
smoothers. Deeproot Wayfinder is a pretty sweet&nbsp;
little guy that can filter and ramp a bit early,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:53.200
and maybe even a bit later too if we can keep a&nbsp;
path clear for him. House Cartographer is a neat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:58.080
card to help us keep up a steady flow of lands to&nbsp;
hand, and our commander’s Station ability gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:02.560
an easy way to trigger survival once attacks&nbsp;
are off the table. Another card I added was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:07.760
Undergrowth Recon, as this deck seems like it&nbsp;
could use a bit more land recursion. Honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:12.160
I think you could even look into adding another&nbsp;
1-2 beyond this, but the other ones out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:15.920
tend to start around the three dollar mark, and&nbsp;
if you’re looking for the best bang for your buck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:19.920
you’re gonna be best off grabbing Spelunking and&nbsp;
some landfall cards as your first upgrades over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:26.160
a dollar. The final nonland card I added is Hugs,&nbsp;
Grisly Guardian. This deck could use both another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:30.720
little boost of draw and another additional land&nbsp;
play enabler, so I suppose you could add Curse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.920
of the Locked Hothouse as an alternative,&nbsp;
but I’ve definitely already given that card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:38.400
plenty of attention on this channel, and Hugs&nbsp;
brings that lovely Badger energy to the table.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:42.880
Those are the 8 nonland swaps, but I’m also&nbsp;
choosing to swap two lands out. For one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:46.880
the Wastes, which I guess was here as a&nbsp;
basic-typed way to pay for World Breaker’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:54.000
ability? Maybe? Let’s go with that. The other land&nbsp;
cut is Dakmor Salvage—dredge is cute, but I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:58.720
think this is actually likely to ever do a lot&nbsp;
outside of being a black tapland. In their place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:03.520
I bring in Twisted Landscape, a pretty high&nbsp;
quality tri-fetch, as well as Kessig Wolf Run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:08.160
which can be a mana sink and a way to give trample&nbsp;
to one of the deck’s large-but-non-trampling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:13.200
creatures. With that, my 10 swaps are done here.&nbsp;
Sometimes I do more than that, but in this case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:18.160
this deck is just looking pretty good, it doesn’t&nbsp;
need much extra help. The base list is a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:22.240
payoff-heavy, which I imagine most players will&nbsp;
figure out at some point, and giving them the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:26.720
option of what to cut and which directions to&nbsp;
push harder in is a reasonable enough task.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:31.440
The new cards for this deck are interesting—on the&nbsp;
one hand my two least-favorite cards in this list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:36.480
were new additions specifically for this deck,&nbsp;
which is a little odd, but some of the other newly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:41.920
printed cards are fantastic and do great things&nbsp;
for this deck. Evendo Brushrazer is a fantastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:46.240
value engine, Horizon Explorer is a much welcome&nbsp;
additional Amulet of Vigor effect with some neat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:51.280
upside, and Planetary Annihilation is a wild&nbsp;
board wipe with a ton of different use cases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:54.400
I love the creative design space this&nbsp;
precon is exploring, and the strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:59.760
and land-fiddling nature of the deck fits the&nbsp;
flavor of Edge of Eternity perfectly to me.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:03.680
Thanks for watching, and thanks to Archidekt&nbsp;
for supporting the channel. Archidekt’s category&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:08.000
system and smooth UI really helps me out in&nbsp;
analyzing decks and making reviews like this one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:12.560
and a link to the final upgraded list is in the&nbsp;
description. I also want to thank my Patrons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:16.400
some of whom are on screen now, and if you feel&nbsp;
like joining them in supporting the channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:20.160
you’ll get access to a bonus video where&nbsp;
I discuss my thoughts on some EoE cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:25.520
for about half an hour. A preview for&nbsp;
that video will start riiiiiight now!

